.TH "bash\-server\-ndjson" "5" "2026\-02\-01" "bash\-server 0.1.0" "bash\-server file formats"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \f[B]NDJSON (Newline\-Delimited JSON)\f[R] format is an alternative
wire framing for the \f[B]bash\-server\f[R](1) v2 protocol.
It carries the same JSON message schemas and channel semantics as the
binary v2 framing (see \f[B]bash\-server\-v2\-protocol\f[R](5)), but
uses a text\-based, line\-oriented envelope that is easier to debug and
generate from scripting languages.
.SH FORMAT
.SH Framing
Each message is a single JSON object serialized on one line and
terminated by LF (0x0A):
.IP
.EX
{\(dqchannel\(dq:N, ...message fields...}\(rsn
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
Each line MUST be valid JSON.
.IP \(bu 2
Each JSON object MUST include a \f[B]\(lqchannel\(rq\f[R] field
(integer, 0\-5).
.IP \(bu 2
No blank lines or comments are permitted between messages.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no explicit maximum line length, but the same 1 MB payload
limit from the binary v2 protocol applies to the serialized JSON.
.SH Channel field
The \f[B]\(lqchannel\(rq\f[R] field replaces the channel byte from the
binary frame header.
It takes the same integer values:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Value
T}@T{
Channel
T}
_
T{
0
T}@T{
CHAN_CONTROL
T}
T{
1
T}@T{
CHAN_COMMAND
T}
T{
2
T}@T{
CHAN_STATE
T}
T{
3
T}@T{
CHAN_OBSERVE
T}
T{
4
T}@T{
CHAN_DEBUG
T}
T{
5
T}@T{
CHAN_PTY
T}
.TE
.PP
All remaining fields in the JSON object are the message payload,
identical to the schemas documented in
\f[B]bash\-server\-json\-messages\f[R](5).
.SH Wire format selection
The wire format is determined once per session during auto\-detection
and cannot change mid\-connection.
Two internal functions manage the format:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]json_set_wire_format(format)\f[R] \(em set the format for the
current session.
\f[I]format\f[R] is \f[B]WIRE_BINARY\f[R] (0) or \f[B]WIRE_NDJSON\f[R]
(1).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]json_get_wire_format()\f[R] \(em return the current format.
.SH Auto\-detection
If the first byte received on a new connection is \f[B]{\f[R] (0x7B) or
\f[B]* (0x0A), NDJSON mode is selected.
Otherwise, the byte is evaluated against the binary v2 and v1 text
rules.
See \f[R]bash\-server\-v2\-protocol**(5) for the complete
auto\-detection algorithm.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SH Authentication handshake
Client sends:
.IP
.EX
{\(dqchannel\(dq:0,\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqauth\(dq,\(dqtoken\(dq:\(dqa3f7c9e2b14d08563fa91e7c4b2d60f8e5a3c7d9012b4f68e3a1c5d7f9b20e46\(dq}
.EE
.PP
Server responds:
.IP
.EX
{\(dqchannel\(dq:0,\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqauth_ok\(dq}
.EE
.SH Evaluating a command
Client:
.IP
.EX
{\(dqchannel\(dq:1,\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqeval\(dq,\(dqcommand\(dq:\(dqecho hello world\(dq}
.EE
.PP
Server (multiple lines):
.IP
.EX
{\(dqchannel\(dq:1,\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqstdout\(dq,\(dqdata\(dq:\(dqaGVsbG8gd29ybGQK\(dq,\(dqencoding\(dq:\(dqbase64\(dq}
{\(dqchannel\(dq:1,\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqcomplete\(dq,\(dqexit_code\(dq:0}
.EE
.SH Variable operations
.IP
.EX
{\(dqchannel\(dq:2,\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqget_var\(dq,\(dqname\(dq:\(dqHOME\(dq}
{\(dqchannel\(dq:2,\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqvar\(dq,\(dqname\(dq:\(dqHOME\(dq,\(dqvalue\(dq:\(dq/home/alice\(dq}
.EE
.SH Ping
.IP
.EX
{\(dqchannel\(dq:0,\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqping\(dq}
{\(dqchannel\(dq:0,\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqpong\(dq}
.EE
.SH Observation event (server push)
.IP
.EX
{\(dqchannel\(dq:3,\(dqlevel\(dq:1,\(dqtype\(dq:\(dqpre_command\(dq,\(dqseq\(dq:42,\(dqtimestamp\(dq:1706640000,\(dqdata\(dq:{\(dqcommand\(dq:\(dqls \-la\(dq,\(dqcwd\(dq:\(dq/home/alice\(dq,\(dqline_number\(dq:1,\(dqis_subshell\(dq:\f[B]false\f[R],\(dqis_async\(dq:\f[B]false\f[R]}}
.EE
.SH NOTES
NDJSON is particularly convenient for:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Shell scripts\f[R] using tools like \f[B]jq\f[R](1) and
\f[B]socat\f[R](1).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Python\f[R] clients using the \f[B]json\f[R] standard library.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Debugging\f[R] with \f[B]nc\f[R](1) or \f[B]ncat\f[R](1).
.PP
The trade\-off compared to binary framing is slightly higher bandwidth
usage due to the JSON envelope and text encoding of the channel field.
